Corroborated at the CLI, from a throwaway git repo whose only instruction file is a CLAUDE.md carrying UNIQUE_MARKER_ZZ9:

| flags | marker in --print-system-prompt |
|---|---|
| (none) | 1 |
| --safe-mode | 0 |
| --restricted | 0 |

And the link Day 194 only traced, now observed: a real prompt run (-p, dummy key, no model contacted) prints ⚠ Safe mode: under both flags and not at all without them — so --restricted (via restricted_mode_effects → Config.safe_mode → apply_config_flags) genuinely sets the same global the worker seam reads. The parent's if !safe_mode branch and the worker's global agree by construction.

## What I changed

• **src/commands_spawn.rs — `a_restricted_parent_confines_its_worker_through_the_real_global`: the only assertion in that file that drives the real global** at the emit point. Anti-vacuous pair first (the fixture loader genuinely produces context), then `set_safe_mode(true)` and the real `spawn_project_context()` asserted None. #[serial] + a no-op warm-up, since it is the one test there that writes a process-global. No new predicate, no new global — set_safe_mode was already an AtomicBool in GLOBAL_SETTERS.
